        "orario": "20:41",
        "timestamp": 1773862888,
        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Kharkiv's opera and ballet theatre performances continue despite war Europe Issued on: 18/03/2026 - 20:41 Ukraine’s second-largest city, Kharkiv, is just 30km from Russia and suffers almost daily bombardment from across the border. But as the years of war have gone by, the city’s cultural institutions have adapted in order to keep working despite the dangers. The national opera and ballet theatre in the city centre stages regular performances. Most recently, to coincide with the four-year anniversary of the invasion, it revived a production last performed on the 23rd of February 2022. This report from Gulliver Cragg and Tarek Kai. Soler i Lecha: Foreign policy of Pedro Sánchez 'widely supported by the Spanish population' 18/03/2026 Spain 'Shield for Europe: We protect not only Ukraine, but the whole of Europe', says Dr Iryna Mudra 18/03/2026 Europe 'Misguided' military operation: Trump’s appeal for Hormuz support draws no commitments from allies 17/03/2026 Middle East US realising it 'needs help from allies even if all-powerful militarily' 16/03/2026 Europe Europe refuses to join Middle East war, weighs options to secure Strait of Hormuz 16/03/2026 Middle East Middle East conflict is not NATO war, but 'entwined' with US position on Ukraine 16/03/2026 Middle East 44% abstention: French local elections 'poorly attended dress rehearsal' for 2027 presidential race 16/03/2026 France Ukraine recovering from Russian strike, concerned Putin is benefiting from Iran war 15/03/2026 Europe Made in UK: Ukrainian drone company to open first UK plant to boost production 13/03/2026 Europe"
